  'storyOrbit.tradizione.imageTitle': 'Family Tradition',
  'storyOrbit.tradizione.imageText': 'Every story is different and ours is authentic, telling of commitment, passion and Italian enterprise. Our journey began in Florence in the summer of 1932, when master gelato maker Idilio Badiani opened his first gelateria. In 1993 the brand was acquired by gelato maker Orazio Pomposi who, together with his two sons Paolo and Patrizio, brought Badiani to new milestones through innovation and creativity applied to flavours and production. Today Badiani is an international presence that upholds the high standards of Florentine gelato tradition, starting with its most famous flavour: Buontalenti. In 2015 we entered the British market with several shops in London and a dedicated laboratory. Our gelato is crafted according to centuries-old Italian tradition: a team of expert gelato makers, led by our Gelato maker Paolo Pomposi, transforms our carefully selected ingredients into the most delicious artisan gelato.',

  'storyOrbit.idea.imageTitle': 'The Idea',
  'storyOrbit.idea.imageText': 'What is the "big idea" behind the Badiani brand that creates a bond with the public and sets us apart from competitors? Badiani 1932 was born in a special city: Florence, which is a global icon and heritage of beauty, craftsmanship and creative genius. The brand draws strength from this legacy. Badiani is first and foremost a Gelateria where every flavour is carefully crafted by a Master Gelato Maker: Paolo Pomposi, who is also one of the owners and the son of a gelato artisan. Badiani embodies the Florentine artisan culture turned into a multi-award-winning product of excellence. Through its signature flavour "Buontalenti", Badiani promises customers a unique and exclusive taste experience that represents not only absolute quality, but the chance to "taste in London a historic and legendary flavour created by a creative genius": Architect Bernardo Buontalenti, the first to invent gelato in Florence during the Renaissance.',

  'storyOrbit.gelato.imageTitle': 'The Culture of Buontalenti',
  'storyOrbit.gelato.imageText': 'Cosimo I de\' Medici, the refined lord of Tuscany, one day around the mid-sixteenth century commissioned Bernardo Buontalenti to organise a special celebration that could impress a delegation from the King of Spain, expected at court to win their support for his plan to annex Siena into the duchy. Bernardo Buontalenti, architect and Creative Genius, was the director of spectacles and fireworks and prepared a programme of festivities never seen before. He organised sumptuous banquets, at the end of which he had a frozen cream served to which he had added a most precious spice arriving from the newly discovered Americas: sugar. Naturally the Spanish delegation was thrilled, to the great satisfaction of the Grand Duke, who had to recruit many cooks to make gelato non-stop. But it was thanks to Catherine de\' Medici, at that time also regent of France, that from the second half of the century gelato spread throughout Europe. From then on, the queen summoned to court the Florentine pastry chefs who had mastered that art and never again failed to offer gelato to her guests — rulers and diplomats who then carried the recipe back to their homelands.',

  'storyOrbit.buontalenti.imageTitle': 'Buontalenti',
  'storyOrbit.buontalenti.imageText': '"Buontalenti" promises customers a unique and exclusive taste experience that represents not only absolute quality, but the chance to "taste in London a historic and legendary flavour created by a creative genius": Architect Bernardo Buontalenti, the first to invent gelato in Florence during the Renaissance.',


